## Claim
Text: "UK Employees unable to work in tier 3 areas will get a combination of Job Support Scheme and Universal Credit, which will mean they get 80% of their wages."
Claim author: Boris Johnson
Claim date: October 21, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Did the UK government have a tiered COVID-19 restriction system in place in October 2020, and was "tier 3" the highest restriction level?
Answer: Yes. According to the search result, Prime Minister Boris Johnson announced a three-tier system of COVID-19 restrictions in October 2020, which came into effect on Wednesday October 14. The system categorized every area of England as either medium, high, or very high risk, with "tier 3" (very high) being the highest restriction level, as evidenced by the fact that the Liverpool City Region was initially the only area placed into this top tier.

Source URL: https://www.cambridgeindependent.co.uk/news/the-three-tier-system-of-covid-restrictions-explained-which-tier-is-my-area-in-9126226/

### What were the rules and restrictions imposed on businesses and employees in tier 3 areas of the UK in October 2020?
Answer: According to the search result, in October 2020, Tier 3 ("very high risk") areas in England faced strict restrictions including the closure of "pubs and bars, betting shops, casinos, adult gaming centres and gyms," bans on "all-but-essential travel," and prevention of overnight stays, with measures implemented in four-week blocks. For employees affected by these closures, the Job Support Scheme (launching November 1) would have the Government pay "two thirds of each employee's salary – up to a maximum of £2,100 a month – if their employer is legally required to close their premises because of restrictions."

Source URL: https://guernseypress.com/news/uk-news/2020/10/12/new-three-tier-covid-restrictions-expected-to-hit-north-hard/

### What was the Job Support Scheme announced by the UK government, and what were its payment terms, employer contribution requirements, and eligibility criteria?
Answer: The Job Support Scheme (JSS) was a UK government support scheme that ran from 1 November 2020 to the end of April 2021, designed to help employers retain employees workingreduced hours. Under its payment terms, eligible employees were required to work a minimum of one third of their usual hours (paid by the employer in the usual way), while the employer and the UK government each paid one third of the wages for unworked hours, leaving the remaining one third of unworked hours unpaid; the government's contribution was capped at £697.92 per month, and crucially, "An employee or worker placed on JSS will usually receive 78% of their usual wages." Regarding employer contributions and eligibility, SMEs (fewer than 250 employees) could claim without financial assessment, while large employers had to demonstrate decreased turnover; the employer was required to pay the Class 1 employer NIC and pension contributions on the wages they paid, employees had to have been on the employer's PAYE payroll on or before 23 September 2020, and employers could not make employees redundant while claiming under the scheme. Notably, the search result does not contain information about the combination of JSS with Universal Credit or any top-up to reach 80% of wages as claimed.

Source URL: https://hanne.co.uk/uk-government-job-support-scheme/

### What amount of financial support did the Job Support Scheme provide to employees, and was there a cap on the wages covered?
Answer: According to the search result, the Job Support Scheme provided 67% of normal salary, with a cap of £2,100 a month maximum. As the source states, "the Job Support Scheme, which will replace it in November, will provide 67% of normal salary up to a maximum of £2,100 a month." This was lower than the previous furlough scheme, which had covered 80% of wages up to £2,500.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147

### What is Universal Credit, how is its payment amount calculated, and what are the maximum and minimum amounts an eligible adult could receive in October 2020?
Answer: The search result explains that Universal Credit is a means-tested benefit paid by the Department for Work and Pensions, which "replaces several existing 'legacy benefits' for working age residents" and combines living costs and housing costs into a single monthly payment, with the amount depending on factors such as "family size, disability and any other income or savings." However, the source does not provide the specific calculation formula (such as the taper rate or work allowance), nor does it give the maximum and minimum payment amounts an eligible adult could receive in October 2020, so the specific figures requested cannot be answered from this result.

Source URL: https://www.towerhamlets.gov.uk/lgnl/advice_and_benefits/benefits/housing_benefit/housing_benefit.aspx

### Were employees in tier 3 areas who were unable to work eligible to receive BOTH the Job Support Scheme payment AND Universal Credit simultaneously?
Answer: Yes, but only for some workers. According to the BBC, "Whether a worker gets Universal Credit on top of the job support scheme depends on a number of factors such as the level of their income, whether they have savings and whether they have children." Specifically, "people whose wages were low enough for them to qualify for Universal Credit before their employers were forced to close, are likely to get at least 80% of their wages," but "the prime minister failed to mention those who do not get UC at all, or only get a bit of it, who would receive less than 80%." Additionally, "a worker who has £16,000 in savings will not qualify for any UC, regardless of any other factors." So while simultaneous eligibility existed, it was not universal among all tier 3 employees unable to work.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147

### When combining the Job Support Scheme payment and Universal Credit, would the total amount received by an employee in a tier 3 area equal 80% of their original wages?
Answer: No, the total would not equal 80% for all employees. According to the BBC, "While that will be the case for some workers, especially those on very low incomes, some workers will get less than 80% under the new scheme." The Job Support Scheme itself only provides 67% of normal salary (capped at £2,100/month), and Universal Credit eligibility depends on factors like income, savings, and dependents. For example, the Institute for Fiscal Studies calculated that a single person with no children who owns their home and earns £11,000 a year would end up with just 73% of their usual income when combining the two benefits, and those with £16,000 in savings would not qualify for any Universal Credit at all.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147

### Does the 80% figure in the claim account for any wage caps, tapering of Universal Credit, or income tax and National Insurance deductions?
Answer: Therefore, the source only partially addresses the question, confirming that the 80% figure is compromised by the £697.92 monthly cap on the government's JSS contribution, but it does not address Universal Credit tapering or tax/NI deductions.

Source URL: https://ogletreedeakins.co.uk/insights-resources/blog-posts/uk-government-introduces-new-job-support-scheme-to-protect-jobs-still-affected-by-the-covid-19-crisis/

### Was the Job Support Scheme active and operational on October 21, 2020, or was it a planned/announced programme scheduled to begin at a later date?
Answer: The Job Support Scheme was **not** active or operational on October 21, 2020. According to the source, it was a planned/announced programme scheduled to begin later. The document explicitly states: "The JSS is available to employers for six months, **from 1 November 2020**" and "This is a temporary scheme, available to employers **from 1 November 2020** for 6 months." Therefore, on the date Boris Johnson made the claim (October 21, 2020), the scheme had been announced but was not yet operational—it was set to launch approximately 11 days later.

Source URL: https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5f8074fb8fa8f50454ce9339/20201009_JSSC_Factsheet_FINAL_EG_1516_002_.pdf

### Did the UK government, the Treasury, or HM Revenue & Customs officially confirm or publish guidance stating that the combination of the Job Support Scheme and Universal Credit would provide 80% of wages to employees in tier 3 areas?
Answer: The Department for Work and Pensions (DWP) confirmed to the BBC that "those on low incomes getting the full entitlement [of Universal Credit] will receive at least 80% of their normal income," but this confirmation was explicitly limited to low-income workers receiving the full UC entitlement — not all employees in tier 3 areas. The search result does not mention any official guidance from the Treasury or HMRC stating that the combination of the Job Support Scheme and Universal Credit would universally provide 80% of wages to all tier 3 workers, and the BBC's fact-check verdict concluded that "some workers will get less than 80% under the new scheme."

Source URL: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147

## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Findings

The evidence confirms that the UK had a tiered COVID-19 restriction system in October 2020, with tier 3 being the highest level. The Job Support Scheme (JSS) was announced but not yet operational on October 21, 2020 (it launched November 1, 2020). Critically, the JSS itself only provided 67% of normal salary (capped at £2,100/month), not 80%. While Universal Credit could be combined with JSS, eligibility depended on factors like income, savings, and dependents—not all employees would qualify. The BBC fact-check explicitly concluded that "some workers will get less than 80% under the new scheme," and the IFS calculated that a single person earning £11,000/year with no children would only receive 73% of their usual income. Workers with £16,000 in savings would not qualify for any UC at all.

## Missing Information

No significant information is lacking. The evidence is comprehensive enough to reach a conclusion.

## Conclusion

The claim is technically true for a subset of workers—specifically those on low incomes who qualify for the full Universal Credit entitlement—but it is misleading because it presents the 80% figure as universal when it does not apply to all employees in tier 3 areas. The claim omits crucial context about savings thresholds (£16,000 disqualifies workers from UC entirely), income levels, and the fact that the JSS itself only covers 67% of wages. Excluding this context creates a significantly different impression of the support available, as many affected workers would receive substantially less than 80% of their wages.

`cherry-picking`

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification

The claim is misleading because it presents the 80% figure as universal when it applies only to a subset of low-income workers. The Job Support Scheme itself only covered 67% of normal salary(capped at £2,100/month) and was not even operational on October 21, 2020 — it launched on November 1, 2020 ([JSS factsheet](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5f8074fb8fa8f50454ce9339/20201009_JSSC_Factsheet_FINAL_EG_1516_002_.pdf)). While Universal Credit could top up the JSS payment to at least 80% for some workers, eligibility depended on income, savings, and dependents — workers with £16,000 in savings would not qualify for any Universal Credit at all, and the Institute for Fiscal Studies calculated that a single person earning £11,000/year with no children would receive only 73% of their usual income, as confirmed by the [BBC](https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147). The DWP's confirmation of the 80% figure was explicitly limited to low-income workers receiving the full UC entitlement, meaning the claim cherry-picks the most favorable scenario while omitting crucial context about caps, savings thresholds, and the scheme's not-yet-active status.